Steam Enhanced, client/loader.js: a menu window's stylesheets in one file.

Why. Steam opens every menu (the right-click menu of a game, Games, View, the Steam menu...) in a
new popup window ("contextmenu_" + the menu's key, created on each open), and Steam Enhanced's
loader puts its ~30 stylesheets into it as one <style blocking="render"> of @imports: the menu
cannot draw until every one of them has been fetched. Measured in a menu window: the imports
take ~80 ms from the first request to the last; Chromium then matches the same rules either way
(3-4 ms for a menu's ~120 elements).

What. Arkada's build (tools/build-se.js, menuBundles) writes the stylesheets a menu window gets
into core/menu.bundle.css (groups "all") and core/menu-tray.bundle.css ("all" + "tray", the plain
"Menu" windows), concatenated in the loader's list order, so the cascade is the same as with the
imports (an @import'ed sheet folds into the importing sheet at its place). The only relative url()
there is core/font.css's fonts/..., and the bundles sit in core/ next to it. In a menu window (not
on the web) the loader then imports the one bundle instead of the files.

For a pull request to Steam Enhanced: the bundling would go into their own build/release step
(or a dev script), with the same MENU_BUNDLES lookup in the loader.

